  "id": "e00057",
  "title": "Liters and Grams Word Problems",
  "intro": "Distinguish the number of containers from the amount in each container.",
  "html": "<h2>Teaching notes for parents and teachers</h2><h3>How to teach it</h3><p>Label both quantities in an equal-group drawing. Name the unit when finding a total or the amount left.</p><h3>Example to work through together</h3><p>Use the model below as you explain the example. Have your student point to the quantities as you discuss them.</p><div class=\"model measurement-model\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Equal amounts in three containers\" style=\"overflow-x:auto\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three containers each hold four liters, for twelve liters altogether\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 210\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:560px\"><title>Three equal amounts, four liters in each</title><g class=\"liter-container\"><path d=\"M35 25v120h110V25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#164f5b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"38\" y=\"65\" width=\"104\" height=\"77\" fill=\"#176e5c\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"113\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"22\">4 L</text></g><g class=\"liter-container\"><path d=\"M195 25v120h110V25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#164f5b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"198\" y=\"65\" width=\"104\" height=\"77\" fill=\"#176e5c\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"113\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"22\">4 L</text></g><g class=\"liter-container\"><path d=\"M355 25v120h110V25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#164f5b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"358\" y=\"65\" width=\"104\" height=\"77\" fill=\"#176e5c\"/><text x=\"410\" y=\"113\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"22\">4 L</text></g><text x=\"280\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#164f5b\" font-size=\"20\">4 L + 4 L + 4 L = 12 L</text></svg><p>Each label gives the amount inside one container. These are schematic containers, not measuring scales: use the labels rather than estimating a volume from the drawing.</p></div><div class=\"model mass-part-model\"><p class=\"sample-equation\">12 g = 5 g used + 7 g left</p><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Twelve grams split into five grams used and seven grams left\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 70\" style=\"display:block;width:100%\"><title>Used and remaining mass make the original amount</title><rect class=\"used-mass\" x=\"0\" y=\"8\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#b74724\"/><rect class=\"remaining-mass\" x=\"150\" y=\"8\" width=\"210\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#176e5c\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"18\">5 g used</text><text x=\"255\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"18\">7 g left</text></svg></div><h3>Ask your student</h3><blockquote><p>Does this number count containers or measure what is inside?</p></blockquote><h3>If your student needs help</h3><p>If the student combines grams with a container count, attach units to each number before calculating.</p>",
  "source": "content/en-US/word-problems-liters-grams.json",
  "origin": "reused"
}
